const abstract = `Learned detectors for network and API threats face two pressures that are usually studied apart: attackers who perturb their traffic to evade the model, and privacy rules that keep organizations from pooling logs. We present FAGI (Federated Adversarial Graph Intrusion detection), which combines a GraphSAGE detector over k-nearest-neighbor flow neighborhoods, GNNGuard edge filtering, adversarial training with projected gradient descent (PGD), and differentially private SGD (DP-SGD) inside federated averaging, and we measure what each component buys and costs. On all eight day files of CICIDS2017 (${int(ds.clean_rows)} flows after cleaning; a stratified sample of ${int(ds.sample_rows)} under 5-fold cross-validation with ${nSeeds} seeds), neighbors come from a reference pool kept apart from the private training set, so DP-SGD gives a per-flow guarantee (ε_{DP} = ${fx(eC)}, δ = 10^{-6}) for the private half of each training fold, conditional on the pool, which is published without protection. Every model exceeds ${Math.floor(100 * Math.min(...E1.models.map((m) => M(m.clean))))}% clean accuracy and a random forest is best. We attack only malicious flows, in feature space without protocol constraints and through each model's own inference path. The attacks, which minimize each model's malicious score rather than stopping at its decision boundary, are benign mimicry, transfer, a retrieval-aware attack that moves a flow toward benign pool neighbors, white-box gradient attacks on the score, and score-based random search, which is also the random forest's direct attack. A model that flags more benign traffic also catches more attacked flows, so we compare robustness with every model held to a ${fprTxt} false-positive rate. At ε_{adv} = 0.5, adversarial training lets a graph detector keep ${pct(mf("fagi_no_dp", "0.5"))}% of attacked flows detected, against ${pct(mf("gnn", "0.5"))}% for an undefended GNN and none for every flat model, PGD-trained ones included; an untrained k-NN vote over the same pool, which uses no private record at all, keeps ${pct(mf("knn", "0.5"))}%, level with the adversarially trained graph detector. FAGI, which adds DP-SGD, keeps ${pct(mf("fagi", "0.5"))}%, level with the undefended GNN at this rate and clearly ahead of it only at a 2% false-positive rate. At its default threshold FAGI appears to be the most robust model (${pct(w("fagi", "0.5"))}%) largely because it flags ${pct(M(B.fagi.fpr), 2)}% of benign flows. At a 1% false-positive rate DP-SGD gives back most of what adversarial training buys (less at 2%), and it costs ${pp(M(AB.fagi_no_dp.clean), M(AB.fagi.clean))} points of clean accuracy; GNNGuard has no measurable effect. ${XC.abstract}The random forest is the most accurate clean detector; against its own query attack it keeps ${pct(mf("rf", "0.1"))}% of attacked flows at ε_{adv} = 0.1 and a 1% false-positive rate, and none at ε_{adv} = 0.5. ${TEMP_TEXT.abstract} Loss-based membership inference is at chance even against an overfit control, and a label-flip canary audit separates the non-private models from FAGI but certifies at most ε ≈ ${fx(Math.max(...CAN.fagi_no_dp.eps_lower_bound_over_splits.values, ...CAN.gnn.eps_lower_bound_over_splits.values), 1)} even for them, so it cannot test the accounted ε_{DP}. ${FED_TEXT.abstract}`;

k.push(...L.abstract(abstract, "adversarial machine learning, differential privacy, federated learning, graph neural networks, intrusion detection, API security."));

// ---------- I ----------
k.push(L.section("I. Introduction"));
k.push(P(`APIs are how cloud services talk to each other, and the OWASP API Security Top 10 {owasp} catalogues the ways that interface gets abused. Salt Security reported a 400% increase in unique API attackers among its customers over six months in its Q1 2023 report {salt}. Verizon's 2024 Data Breach Investigations Report (DBIR) found that exploitation of vulnerabilities as an initial breach vector rose by 180% in a year {dbir}, and CISA's advisory AA24-038A documented state-sponsored actors holding persistent access to U.S. critical infrastructure for years {cisa}. Machine-learning detectors are a natural response, but two problems follow them into deployment. Attackers adapt their traffic to evade a learned model {szegedy,grosse,apruzzese22}. Organizations, meanwhile, cannot pool the logs a detector would learn from {mothukuri}.`));
k.push(P(`Each problem has a defense. Adversarial training hardens a model against bounded perturbations {madry}. GNNGuard prunes suspicious edges in graph models {gnnguard}. DP-SGD bounds what a model reveals about any one training record {abadi}, and federated averaging trains without moving data {fedavg}. Stacking them is tempting, and each combination claims the benefits of all its parts. What that costs is rarely measured.`));
k.push(P(`This paper measures that cost. FAGI builds attributed graphs from network flows or API sessions, classifies them with a two-layer GraphSAGE {sage}, and layers on GNNGuard, PGD training, and DP-SGD with federated averaging. We test it on a stratified sample drawn from all eight CICIDS2017 day files {cicids} and on a synthetic API-session dataset. For each component we ask what it buys against a stated attacker, and what it costs.`));
k.push(P(`The contributions are: (1) a construction in which graph neighbors come from a reference pool that is disjoint from the private training set, so that DP-SGD gives a valid per-record guarantee for a neighborhood-based detector, together with a measurement of what the pool costs; (2) an evaluation that attacks malicious flows only, through each model's own inference path, with attacks that minimize each model's score, including a retrieval-aware attack for neighborhood models and a query attack for the random forest, and that reports the worst case both at each model's default threshold and at a matched false-positive rate; (3) fold-level paired tests and win counts for the main comparisons and the component ablation, a privacy-utility sweep, and controls that separate DP's noise from clipping and from untuned hyperparameters and that compare DP-SGD with models that use no private data; (4) empirical privacy measurements with a membership-inference attack and planted canaries, which detect DP-SGD's effect but are too weak to test the accounted ε; (5) a file-order split that trains on the first rows of each day file and tests on the last; and (6) a federated experiment that tests generalization to an organization whose data never enters training, with DP and federation separated.`));
k.push(P(`The results do not crown a single design. Compared at a matched false-positive rate, adversarial training is the component that keeps a graph detector working as the attack budget grows, and DP gives most of that robustness back. A random forest is the most accurate clean detector, but a query attack on its scores lowers its recall at ε_{adv} = 0.1 to ${pct(mf("rf", "0.1"))}% at a matched 1% false-positive rate and to zero at 0.5. GNNGuard has no measurable effect, and DP also lowers accuracy, mostly on rare attacks. A k-NN vote over the reference pool, with no training and no access to private records, is as robust as FAGI without DP and more robust than FAGI. At each model's default threshold several of these comparisons look different, because the models flag very different amounts of benign traffic; we report both and draw conclusions from the matched comparison. Section II reviews related work, Section III states the threat model, Section IV describes FAGI, Section V reports the evaluation, Section VI the ablation, a case study, and controls for DP-SGD and the reference pool, Section VII discusses what the evidence supports and its limitations, and Section VIII concludes.`));

// ---------- II ----------
k.push(L.section("II. Related Work"));
k.push(L.subsection("A. API Security and Evaluation Practice"));
k.push(P(`Ejaz catalogued cloud API management anti-patterns such as excessive trust delegation and missing schema validation {ejaz}, and supply-chain compromises add structural risk {ohm,ladisa}. Sommer and Paxson explained why detectors trained on one traffic distribution often fail on another {sommer}. Arp et al. list evaluation pitfalls in security ML, including data snooping and sampling bias {arp}. CICIDS2017 {cicids} is the most used benchmark of its kind, and it has documented labeling problems {engelen}.`));
k.push(L.subsection("B. Graph Neural Networks for Intrusion Detection"));
k.push(P(`Graph neural networks learn from structure and attributes through message passing {sage}. E-GraphSAGE applies GraphSAGE to flow graphs for network intrusion detection systems (NIDS) {egraphsage}, and graph learning has worked for related tasks such as security patch detection {graphspd}. Wang et al. re-evaluated graph NIDS and found that published results overstate what these models achieve under careful evaluation {wang25}. Neural detectors include autoencoders over flow features {kitsune} and CNNs over connection records rendered as images {li_cnn}. CNNs over raw bytes have also been used for malware {raff}.`));
k.push(L.subsection("C. Adversarial Machine Learning in Security"));
k.push(P(`Small, targeted input changes flip deep classifiers {szegedy}, in malware {grosse} and in network detection {apruzzese22,sheatsley}. But gradient attacks in feature space are not the attacks practitioners face {apruzzese23}, and attacks that respect problem-space constraints are a stricter test {pierazzi,zhan25}. Madry et al. formalized PGD adversarial training {madry}. Defenses with non-differentiable parts can mask gradients {athalye}. Evaluations should therefore use adaptive attacks designed against the specific defense {tramer}, along with strong parameter-free attacks {autoattack}.`));
k.push(L.subsection("D. Payload Semantics"));
k.push(P(`Language models pre-trained on security text {securebert} and learned embeddings of system API call sequences {api2vec} have been used to represent payloads and call sequences. Kim et al. document the throughput cost of LLM features in payload-based detection {kim25}. Our payload signal is a deliberately simple keyword count, so we can isolate its effect.`));
k.push(L.subsection("E. Differential Privacy and Federated Learning"));
k.push(P(`DP-SGD clips each example's gradient and adds Gaussian noise {abadi}. Rényi DP gives tight composition {mironov}, and is converted to (ε, δ) with the bound of Balle et al. {balle} as implemented in Opacus {opacus}. Tuning hyperparameters on private data consumes privacy budget {papernot_tuning}. Graphs add a complication: one node can appear in many neighborhoods, and node-level guarantees need bounded degree or aggregation perturbation {daigavane,gap}. Our reference-pool construction avoids the issue by never placing a private record in another record's neighborhood. DP-SGD also costs more accuracy on under-represented classes {bagdasaryan}. Membership inference measures leakage empirically, with shadow models {shokri}, loss thresholds {yeom}, or per-example calibration {carlini}. Planting canaries audits DP training directly and yields an empirical lower bound on ε {jagielski}. Federated averaging trains without moving data {fedavg} but gives no formal privacy on its own {mothukuri}, and robust aggregation against poisoning is an active area {sanjalawe}.`));

// ---------- III ----------
k.push(L.section("III. Threat Model"));
k.push(L.subsection("A. Adversaries"));
k.push(P(`We consider three adversaries {papernot}. The evasion adversary controls its own malicious traffic and changes the features of its flows or sessions so that the detector labels them benign. It cannot change other users' traffic or the reference pool. We give it white-box access to the model and to the retrieval procedure, which is stronger than the limited-knowledge attacker of most deployments. The inference adversary sees the trained model, as a federation member or the aggregation server would, and tries to learn whether a given record was in an organization's private training data. The poisoning adversary controls federation members and sends malicious updates. We neither defend against it nor evaluate it.`));
k.push(L.subsection("B. Privacy Unit"));
k.push(P(`The protected unit is one record of an organization's private training set: one flow for CICIDS2017, one session for API data. Every training example is the ego graph of exactly one private record, with neighbors drawn from a reference pool that contains no private records. Adding or removing a private record therefore changes exactly one clipped per-example gradient, and DP-SGD's guarantee applies per record. The reference pool is not protected. It plays the role of data an organization already shares or has consent to use, and in our experiments it is a held-out half of each training fold. Feature standardization for CICIDS2017 is fit on the pool. API features are divided by fixed per-feature scales computed once from a public calibration sample, a separate run of the original generator that is never evaluated (the hard generator uses the same scales). Several things fall outside the guarantee. (i) The reference pool, which in our experiments is half of each training fold, is published in the clear; the guarantee covers a record of the private set, conditional on the split that put it there. (ii) The dataset size sets the sampling rate and the number of steps, and federated weighting reveals it. (iii) Preprocessing on the whole dataset (de-duplication, constant-feature removal, the stratified sample) and hyperparameters carried over from earlier work are not accounted for. (iv) Released checkpoints carry no guarantee, because their noise and sampling come from published seeds. ${XO ? `DP also does not hide what the pool reveals about a private flow's surroundings: because the pool is a random half of the same traffic, ${pct(M(XO["private_within_0.1"]), 0)}% of private flows have a pool flow within L∞ distance 0.1 of them, the small attack budget, and ${pct(M(XO["private_within_0.5"]), 0)}% within 0.5, almost the same as for test flows (${pct(M(XO["test_within_0.1"]), 0)}% and ${pct(M(XO["test_within_0.5"]), 0)}%).` : "DP also does not hide what the pool reveals about a private flow's surroundings."} The guarantee is per record, not per host or user.`));

// ---------- IV ----------
k.push(L.section("IV. FAGI Architecture"));
k.push(L.subsection("A. Graph Construction"));
k.push(P(`For API sessions, each of 12 endpoints is a node, and each pair of consecutive calls adds an edge in both directions. A node carries six features: call count, mean duration, mean response size, maximum privilege level, write ratio, and a payload suspicion score that counts tokens from a fixed lexicon of SQL keywords, privilege terms, and export terms. For flows, each query flow becomes the center of an ego graph with its k = ${cfg.k} nearest neighbors from the reference pool, in standardized feature space. The center connects to every neighbor, and each node also connects to its ${cfg.k_inner} nearest nodes within the graph. The payload score does not exist for flows, so on CICIDS2017 all graph models see the same ${E1.n_features} features.`));
k.push(L.subsection("B. Detector and Defenses"));
k.push(P(`The detector has two GraphSAGE layers with mean aggregation and 64 and 32 hidden units {sage}, ReLU, dropout 0.25, and a linear output layer, with 13,090 parameters on flows and 5,026 on API sessions (4,898 without the payload feature). Session graphs are classified from the mean node embedding and flow graphs from the center node. Before each forward pass, GNNGuard-style filtering removes edges whose endpoint features have cosine similarity below τ = ${cfg.gnnguard_tau} {gnnguard}, recomputed on whatever features the model receives, including perturbed ones. Adversarial training minimizes ½L(x) + ½L(x + δ*), where δ* comes from PGD with K_{p} = ${cfg.pgd_train_steps} steps, a random start, step 2.5ε/K_{p}, and ε_{adv} = ${cfg.pgd_eps} {madry}, applied only to nodes the adversary controls.`));
k.push(L.subsection("C. Private Federated Training"));
k.push(P(`Each organization trains locally with DP-SGD {abadi}: every private record enters a batch independently with probability q, each record's gradient of the combined clean and adversarial loss is clipped to norm C = ${cfg.dp_clip}, Gaussian noise with standard deviation σC is added to the sum, and the result is divided by the expected batch size before an Adam step. Per-example gradients are exact. Adam only post-processes the noisy gradient, so keeping its state across rounds does not change the guarantee. Privacy loss is tracked with a Rényi-DP accountant over orders from 1.1 to 1024 {mironov,opacus} and converted at δ = 10^{-6} {balle}. The server averages client models weighted by local dataset size {fedavg}. Algorithm 1 gives the procedure; centralized training is the case of one organization and one round.`));
k.push(L.spacer());
k.push(L.algorithm("**Algorithm 1:** FAGI training with DP-SGD", [
  "**Input:** organizations 1..m with private data D_{i} and reference pool P_{i};",
  "   rounds R; local epochs E; rate q; noise σ; clip C; budget ε_{adv}, steps K_{p}",
  "1: initialize global weights W_{0}; Adam state S_{i} ← ∅ for each i",
  "2: **for** r = 1..R **do**",
  "3:   **for** each organization i **in parallel do**",
  "4:     W ← W_{r−1}; resume Adam from S_{i}",
  "5:     **repeat** ⌈E/q⌉ times:",
  "6:       B ← each record of D_{i} independently with prob. q",
  "7:       **for** each record x ∈ B **do**",
  "8:         G ← ego graph of x with neighbors from P_{i}",
  "9:         δ* ← PGD(f_{W}, G; ε_{adv}, K_{p})  on the adversary's nodes",
  "10:        g_{x} ← ∇_{W}[ ½L(f_{W}(G), y) + ½L(f_{W}(G+δ*), y) ]",
  "11:        g_{x} ← g_{x} / max(1, ‖g_{x}‖_{2} / C)",
  "12:      W ← Adam(W, (Σ_{x} g_{x} + N(0, σ^{2}C^{2}I)) / (q|D_{i}|))",
  "13:      record one step at (q, σ) in the accountant",
  "14:    W_{i} ← W; save Adam state to S_{i}",
  "15:  W_{r} ← Σ_{i} (|D_{i}| / Σ_{j}|D_{j}|) W_{i}",
  "16: **return** W_{R} and ε_{DP,i} for each organization",
]));
k.push(L.caption("f_W applies GNNGuard to its input graph on every call. At inference, a query flow's neighbors come from the reference pool of the organization that runs the detector; in centralized CICIDS2017 experiments, from the fold's single pool.", false));
k.push(L.subsection("D. Cost"));
k.push(P(`Per-example gradients and PGD dominate cost. On a two-core CPU, one CICIDS2017 fold (${int(nP)} private training graphs, ${cfg.epochs} epochs) takes about 15 seconds for the undefended GNN and two to six minutes for FAGI. Inference is one nearest-neighbor query against the reference pool and one forward pass over a nine-node graph.`));

// ---------- V ----------
k.push(L.section("V. Evaluation"));
k.push(L.subsection("A. Data and Protocol"));
k.push(P(`**CICIDS2017.** We use all eight day files of the MachineLearningCSV release {cicids} (${int(ds.raw_rows)} flows, 15 labels). We remove ${int(ds.dropped_nonfinite)} flows with non-finite values, ${int(ds.dropped_duplicates)} exact duplicates, a duplicated header column, and ${ds.dropped_constant_features.length} constant features, leaving ${int(ds.clean_rows)} flows and ${E1.n_features} features. Mis-encoded Web Attack labels are normalized; ${int(ds.rows_with_conflicting_labels)} flows share features with a flow of another label and are kept {engelen}. Per-example DP-SGD and adaptive attacks on two million flows were not feasible on our hardware, so we draw a stratified sample: up to ${int(ds.sample_per_class_cap)} flows from each attack label (all flows of rarer ones) and an equal number of benign flows, ${int(ds.sample_rows)} in total. The task is binary. We use 5-fold cross-validation stratified on the original label, with ${nSeeds} seeds per fold, and a file-order split described in Section V-G. Each training fold is split in half, stratified, into a private training set (${int(nP)} flows) and a reference pool (${int(nR)} flows). Features are transformed with sign(x)·log(1 + |x|) and standardized with pool statistics.`));
k.push(P(`**Synthetic API sessions.** The generator produces 4,000 sessions across five organizations with four attack types, each present in three organizations. In the original generator, attack sessions always carry tokens that benign sessions never do, so we also use a harder variant. In it, 30% of benign sessions add one to three admin, export, or query calls with admin, export, SQL, or filter tokens, and each attack call carries ordinary tokens with probability 0.5 (camouflaged exfiltration calls also lose their enlarged responses). Results on synthetic data are a sanity study of the API setting, not evidence about real APIs. They use 5-fold cross-validation with one seed.`));
k.push(P(`**Models.** On CICIDS2017 we compare a random forest (100 trees, raw features), a k-NN majority vote over the same ${cfg.k} pool neighbors the graph models see, an MLP (128-64), the same MLP with PGD training, the PGD-trained MLP with DP-SGD, a 1D CNN over the feature vector (two convolutions with 32 channels, max-pooling to 8, a 64-unit layer), an undefended GNN, and FAGI. The private MLP needs no pool, so it trains with DP-SGD on the whole training fold (${int(nP + nR)} flows); it is the pool-free private baseline. Neural models train for ${cfg.epochs} epochs with Adam, expected batch size ${cfg.batch_size}, learning rate ${cfg.lr} for graph models and ${cfg.flat_lr} for flat ones, and weight decay 10^{-4}. These settings were carried over from an earlier, unpublished version of this work and from common GraphSAGE practice rather than searched on these folds. That earlier version also used CICIDS2017, so the settings are not entirely free of selection, and a strict accounting would charge some privacy budget for that choice {papernot_tuning}. For FAGI, q = ${cfg.batch_size}/${int(nP)} = ${q.toFixed(4)} and training runs ${int(steps)} steps.`));
k.push(P(`**Attacks.** We attack ${int(split.attacked_malicious_per_fold)} randomly chosen malicious test flows per fold, the same flows for every model, and leave benign flows unperturbed. Every attack keeps the flow within an L∞ budget ε_{adv} of the original, and for each flow we keep the lowest malicious score any attack reaches, so one run serves every decision threshold. The suite has five parts. *Benign mimicry* moves the flow toward each of its 32 nearest benign pool flows, as far as the budget allows; each of the 32 resulting points is a mimicry candidate. *Transfer* replays examples found against other models: the stored PGD examples of the MLP and adaptive-PGD examples of the GNN, and the best examples of the stronger attacks below against the PGD-trained MLP, the GNN, both FAGI variants, and the k-NN vote. A *neighborhood attack*, shared by every model of a fold, runs ${SUITE.nbr_steps} steps of sign-gradient descent on the sum of distances to the ${SUITE.nbr_benign} nearest benign pool flows minus the sum of distances to the ${SUITE.nbr_malicious} nearest malicious ones, from one random start and from the two nearest mimicry candidates; it moves the flow to where its retrieved neighbors are benign, which is what both the vote and the graph models read. *Gradient attacks on the score* use the logit margin rather than cross-entropy, so they do not stall once a flow is confidently classified. Flat neural models get ${SUITE.flat_steps} steps with ${SUITE.flat_restarts} starts, together with the stored ${cfg.pgd_eval_steps}-step PGD. Graph models are attacked through their own retrieval: the stored PGD followed by re-retrieval and adaptive PGD (${cfg.pgd_eval_steps} steps, ${cfg.pgd_restarts} random restarts, neighbors re-retrieved at every step), and a further ${SUITE.graph_steps}-step margin attack from the best example found so far and from the clean flow. Within each step the gradient holds the retrieved neighbors fixed, an approximation in the style of BPDA (backward-pass differentiable approximation) {athalye}. For the k-NN vote the gradient comes from a soft vote that weights the nearest ${SUITE.knn_candidates} pool flows by distance, while the true vote is checked at every step. *Score-based random search* starts from the best example found and proposes random sign flips on a shrinking set of features, alternating with copying features from a mimicry candidate, keeping a proposal when the queried score falls. It needs only scores, so it doubles as a check on the gradient attacks, and it is the random forest's direct attack, with ${int(SUITE.rs_iters_rf)} queries per flow (${int(SUITE.rs_iters_flat)} for flat neural models and ${int(SUITE.rs_iters_retrieval)} for the pool-based models, for which Section V-C reports a run with 1,000). Gradient attacks use a cosine step schedule from ${SUITE.step_max} ε_{adv} to ${SUITE.step_min} ε_{adv}, and every attack stops on a flow once its score falls below ${SUITE.score_floor} (${SUITE.score_floor_rf} for the random forest), below every threshold we evaluate. A flow counts as detected only if every attack against that model fails, and we report this worst-case attack recall; the final score of each flow's best example is recomputed through the deployed inference path. PGD on the fixed, unperturbed neighborhood is reported separately and excluded from the worst case, because a deployed model never sees that graph. Budgets ε_{adv} are L∞ distances in standardized log-feature units; a budget of 0.1 corresponds to scaling each raw feature by a factor between roughly e^{−0.1s} and e^{0.1s}, where s is that feature's standard deviation in log space.`));
k.push(P(`**Statistics and reporting.** The seeds of a fold share its test set, its private/pool split, and its attacked flows, so fold-seed pairs are not independent and the fold is our unit of comparison. We average each model over seeds within a fold and compare models with paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests over the five fold means. With five folds the smallest attainable two-sided p is 0.0625, so we report how many of the five folds favor each model. Folds also share training data, so even these tests are optimistic, and we treat win counts as descriptive. Seed-0 ablations are reported the same way. Percentages are rounded to one decimal place (two for clean accuracy and FPR), and differences are computed before rounding. Every cached trained model and attack result is keyed by a hash of the configuration and of the training and attack source files, so results computed in separate sessions cannot mix code versions.`));

k.push(L.subsection("B. Clean Detection"));
k.push(...L.tableTitle("I", `CICIDS2017: clean detection and worst-case attack recall (%, mean ± std over 5 folds × ${nSeeds} seeds)`));
const shortName = { mlp_dp: "MLP + PGD + DP", knn: "k-NN vote", mlp_at: "MLP + PGD" };
k.push(L.table(["Method", "Clean acc.", "FPR", "Recall ε_{adv}=0.1", "Recall ε_{adv}=0.5", "ε_{DP}"],
  E1.models.map((b) => ({ bold: b.key === "fagi", cells: [shortName[b.key] || b.name, pm(b.clean, 2), pct(M(b.fpr), 2),
    pm(b["worst_recall_eps0.1"]), pm(b["worst_recall_eps0.5"]), b.epsilon ? fx(M(b.epsilon)) : "—"] })),
  [1290, 1000, 520, 920, 920, 450]));
k.push(L.caption(`All values at each model's default threshold; Table III compares models at a matched false-positive rate. Recall: share of attacked malicious flows detected under the worst of all attacks against that model. k-NN statistics are over fold means. FPR: false-positive rate on benign test flows. δ = 10^{-6}; the private MLP's ε_{DP} is lower because it trains on twice as many records, and its feature scaler is fit on half of its own training records, which its ε_{DP} does not cover.`, false));
k.push(P(`Table I shows that clean detection on CICIDS2017 is close to solved for every model: the random forest reaches ${pct(M(B.rf.clean), 2)}% with a ${pct(M(B.rf.fpr), 2)}% false-positive rate, the k-NN vote ${pct(M(B.knn.clean), 2)}%, the undefended GNN ${pct(M(B.gnn.clean), 2)}%, and FAGI ${pct(M(B.fagi.clean), 2)}%. The k-NN result is a warning about the benchmark, not only a baseline: random cross-validation places near-duplicate flows from the same attack burst on both sides of the split {arp}, so neighborhood methods do well almost by construction; Section V-G tests this. FAGI trails the GNN by ${pp(-T1.fagi_vs_gnn_acc.mean_diff, 0, 2)} points (lower in ${T1.fagi_vs_gnn_acc.wins_b} of ${T1.fagi_vs_gnn_acc.n} folds), almost all of it from DP.`));
k.push(P(`The reference pool has its own cost, because half of each training fold is no longer used for training. A non-private GNN trained on the whole fold, with neighbors from the whole fold, reaches ${pct(M(FP.clean), 2)}% (seed 0), against ${pct(M(AB.gnn.clean), 2)}% for the same model with the pool split, a cost of ${pp(M(FP.clean), M(AB.gnn.clean), 2)} points. The pool-free alternative is a private flat model: the PGD-trained MLP with DP-SGD on all training flows reaches ${pct(M(B.mlp_dp.clean), 2)}% at ε_{DP} = ${fx(eM)}, ${pp(T1.fagi_vs_mlp_dp_acc.mean_diff, 0, 2)} points below FAGI (lower in ${T1.fagi_vs_mlp_dp_acc.wins_a} of ${T1.fagi_vs_mlp_dp_acc.n} folds). This is not a matched comparison. The MLP's feature scaler is fit on half of its own training flows, which its guarantee does not cover, and its ε_{DP} is lower than FAGI's, whose ε_{DP} = ${fx(eC)} covers only the private half while the pool is published in full. We therefore do not claim that the pool buys clean accuracy. The difference it does make is robustness, discussed in Section V-C: the private MLP, like the non-private PGD-trained MLP, keeps almost nothing at ε_{adv} = 0.5, so that gap comes from the flat architecture, not from DP.`));
k.push(L.figure(path.join(REPO, "figures/fig1_cicids_benchmark.png"), 3.1, 2.36));
k.push(L.caption("Fig. 1. Clean accuracy and worst-case attack recall at ε_{adv} = 0.1 on CICIDS2017, at each model's default threshold. MLP PGD: PGD-trained MLP; MLP DP: the same with DP-SGD on all training flows."));
const rareRows = rare.filter((r) => PC("gnn")[r]);
k.push(P(`FAGI's losses relative to the undefended GNN fall on the smaller attack classes (Table II). The table does not separate DP from PGD training and GNNGuard, but the ablation (Table VII) attributes almost all of FAGI's loss in overall accuracy to DP. A class with few examples contributes few clipped gradients to sums that carry the same noise as everything else, which is the disparate impact Bagdasaryan et al. describe {bagdasaryan}. The intervals are wide because these labels have few test flows; Table II gives the counts.`));
k.push(...L.tableTitle("II", "Recall on rare CICIDS2017 labels (seed 0, pooled over folds; 95% Wilson interval)"));
k.push(L.table(["Label (n)", "RF", "GNN", "FAGI"],
  rareRows.map((r) => [`${r.replace("Web Attack - ", "").replace("Sql", "SQL")} (${PC("gnn")[r].n})`,
    ...["rf", "gnn", "fagi"].map((m) => `${pct(PC(m)[r].recall, 0)} [${pct(PC(m)[r].ci95[0], 0)}–${pct(PC(m)[r].ci95[1], 0)}]`)]),
  [1550, 1080, 1080, 1080]));
k.push(L.spacer());

k.push(L.subsection("C. Robustness"));
k.push(P(`Table I and this subsection first report recall at each model's default threshold; the matched-FPR comparison follows. At ε_{adv} = 0.1 the MLP detects ${pct(w("mlp", "0.1"))}% of attacked flows, the CNN ${pct(w("cnn", "0.1"))}%, and the random forest ${pct(w("rf", "0.1"))}%. The forest's lack of gradients does not protect it: mimicry and transfer alone leave ${pct(stD("rf", "0.1"))}% detected, adding the neighborhood attack and stronger transfer leaves ${pct(rsq[0])}%, and random search on its scores lowers that to ${pct(rsq[1])}% after 100 queries and ${pct(rsq[5])}% after ${int(SUITE.rs_iters_rf)}, gaining under a point in the last thousand. Fig. 1 shows these values. PGD training holds the MLP at this budget (${pct(w("mlp_at", "0.1"))}%, and ${pct(w("mlp_dp", "0.1"))}% with DP-SGD) but not at ε_{adv} = 0.5 (${pct(w("mlp_at", "0.5"))}% and ${pct(w("mlp_dp", "0.5"))}%). The undefended GNN (${pct(w("gnn", "0.1"))}%), FAGI (${pct(w("fagi", "0.1"))}%), and the k-NN vote (${pct(w("knn", "0.1"))}%) all hold at 0.1; the GNN and FAGI are level (${pct(w("gnn", "0.1"))}% and ${pct(w("fagi", "0.1"))}%; ${folds(T1.fagi_vs_gnn_w01)} favor FAGI).`));
k.push(P(`At the default threshold the undefended GNN falls to ${pct(w("gnn", "0.5"))}% at ε_{adv} = 0.5. FAGI keeps ${pct(w("fagi", "0.5"))}%, ahead of the GNN in every fold (paired difference ${pp(T1.fagi_vs_gnn_w05.mean_diff, 0)} points), and FAGI without DP keeps ${pct(w("fagi_no_dp", "0.5"))}%. At this threshold DP seems to help at 0.5, raising recall by ${pp(T3["fagi_vs_fagi_no_dp_eps0.5"].mean_diff, 0)} points in ${folds(T3["fagi_vs_fagi_no_dp_eps0.5"])}, while it lowers recall at 0.1 by ${pp(0, T3["fagi_vs_fagi_no_dp_eps0.1"].mean_diff)}; the matched-FPR analysis below shows that the gain is a threshold effect. The k-NN vote keeps ${pct(w("knn", "0.5"))}%, and FAGI is ahead of it in ${folds(T1.fagi_vs_knn_w05)} by ${pp(T1.fagi_vs_knn_w05.mean_diff, 0)} points, so at the default threshold the two are level.`));
k.push(P(`How the attack treats retrieval matters. Among the stored attacks on FAGI at ε_{adv} = 0.5, PGD on the fixed neighborhood leaves ${pct(a("fagi", "pgd_fixed_graph", "0.5"))}% of attacked flows detected, the same perturbation followed by re-retrieval leaves ${pct(a("fagi", "pgd_reretrieved", "0.5"))}%, and adaptive PGD leaves ${pct(a("fagi", "pgd_adaptive", "0.5"))}%; benign mimicry, which uses no gradient, leaves ${pct(a("fagi", "mimicry32", "0.5"))}%. Holding the neighborhood fixed, as evaluations of graph detectors such as E-GraphSAGE {egraphsage} do, overstates PGD's recall on FAGI by ${pp(a("fagi", "pgd_fixed_graph", "0.5"), a("fagi", "pgd_reretrieved", "0.5"))} points, and the other attacks lower it by a further ${pp(a("fagi", "pgd_reretrieved", "0.5"), w("fagi", "0.5"))}. Gradient-free attacks beating white-box gradients is the pattern Athalye et al. warn of {athalye,tramer}, so the suite adds attacks that target retrieval and the score directly. Taken alone, the neighborhood attack is not the strongest (it leaves ${pct(fam("fagi", "neighborhood", "0.5"))}% of FAGI's flows detected), but it supplies starting points that the margin attack carries further. Against the stored attacks only, FAGI keeps ${pct(stD("fagi", "0.5"))}%, the GNN ${pct(stD("gnn", "0.5"))}%, and the k-NN vote ${pct(stD("knn", "0.5"))}% at the default threshold; with the full suite they keep ${pct(w("fagi", "0.5"))}%, ${pct(w("gnn", "0.5"))}%, and ${pct(w("knn", "0.5"))}%. Score-based random search, started from the best gradient example, lowers no pool-based model's recall at 0.5 by more than ${pct(Math.max(...["gnn", "fagi_no_dp", "fagi", "knn"].map((k) => fam(k, "score_attack", "0.5") - fam(k, "random_search", "0.5"))), 1)} points, which is evidence that the gradient attacks are not masked. On seed 0, the stored adaptive PGD keeps lowering FAGI's recall with more restarts (${pct(rcv("fagi", 1))}%, ${pct(rcv("fagi", 2))}%, and ${pct(rcv("fagi", 5))}% with one, two, and five), while the full suite is already below all three.`));
k.push(P(`These recalls are measured at each model's default threshold, so a model that flags more benign traffic also catches more attacked flows. FAGI flags ${pct(M(B.fagi.fpr), 2)}% of benign flows, more than any other main model except the private MLP. Table III therefore moves every model's threshold until it flags ${fprTxt} of benign test flows, breaking tied scores at random so that discrete scorers such as the k-NN vote reach the target. Because every attack minimizes the score, the same attacked examples are optimized for any threshold. This threshold is an oracle choice, the same for every model. Calibrating it instead on the benign flows of the public reference pool, each excluded from its own neighborhood, gives realized test false-positive rates of ${pct(Math.min(...poolOK.map((k) => M(FX[k].pool_realized_test_fpr))), 1)}–${pct(Math.max(...poolOK.map((k) => M(FX[k].pool_realized_test_fpr))), 1)}% and changes no recall at ε_{adv} = 0.5 by more than ${pct(Math.max(...poolOK.map((k) => Math.abs(M(FX[k]["pool_0.5"]) - M(FX[k]["fixed_0.01_0.5"])))), 1)} points. The private MLP is the exception: it trains on the pool, so for it this calibration is neither held out nor covered by its guarantee, and we leave it out.`));
k.push(...L.tableTitle("III", `Worst-case attack recall at a matched ${fprTxt} false-positive rate (%, 5 folds × ${nSeeds} seeds)`));
const fxRows = [["rf", "Random Forest"], ["knn", "k-NN vote"], ["mlp", "MLP"], ["mlp_at", "MLP + PGD"], ["mlp_dp", "MLP + PGD + DP"], ["cnn", "1D-CNN"], ["gnn", "GNN Baseline"], ["fagi_no_dp", "FAGI w/o DP"], ["fagi", "FAGI"]];
k.push(L.table(["Method", "FPR at default", "ε_{adv}=0.1", "ε_{adv}=0.5", "ε_{adv}=0.5, stored attacks", "ε_{adv}=0.5, 2% FPR"],
  fxRows.map(([key, name]) => ({ bold: key === "fagi", cells: [name, pct(M(FX[key].fpr_default), 2), pm(FX[key]["fixed_0.1"]), pm(FX[key]["fixed_0.5"]), pct(st(key, "0.5")), pct(mfx(key, "0.02", "0.5"))] })),
  [1250, 750, 850, 850, 700, 700]));
k.push(L.caption("k-NN statistics are over fold means. Stored attacks: mimicry, the model's own stored gradient examples, and the two stored transfer sources, without the neighborhood, margin, strong-transfer and random-search attacks. The last column uses the full suite at a 2% false-positive rate.", false));
const pw = (t) => `${t.pair_wins_a} of ${t.pairs} fold-seed pairs`;
k.push(P(`Matching the false-positive rate changes the ranking. At ε_{adv} = 0.5, FAGI without DP keeps ${pct(mf("fagi_no_dp", "0.5"))}%, ahead of the undefended GNN (${pct(mf("gnn", "0.5"))}%) in all five folds, so adversarial training more than doubles the graph detector's worst-case recall and brings it to the level of an untrained vote over the same neighbors: PGD training stops the attacker from exploiting the center node's features, and the model then follows its retrieved neighbors. The k-NN vote keeps ${pct(mf("knn", "0.5"))}%, level with FAGI without DP (${folds(FT["fagi_no_dp_vs_knn_fixed_0.5"])} favor FAGI without DP, ${pv(FT["fagi_no_dp_vs_knn_fixed_0.5"].p)}). FAGI with DP keeps ${pct(mf("fagi", "0.5"))}%, ${pp(0, FT["fagi_vs_fagi_no_dp_fixed_0.5"].mean_diff)} points below its non-private version in all five folds, and level with the GNN: ${pp(FT["fagi_vs_gnn_fixed_0.5"].mean_diff, 0)} points above it, ahead in ${folds(FT["fagi_vs_gnn_fixed_0.5"])} (${pv(FT["fagi_vs_gnn_fixed_0.5"].p)}), a difference within what a stronger attack can change (Section V-C); at ε_{adv} = 0.1 it falls below the GNN in every fold, ${pct(mf("fagi", "0.1"))}% against ${pct(mf("gnn", "0.1"))}%. The k-NN vote is ahead of FAGI in all five folds at 0.5. Every flat model keeps nothing at 0.5. The random forest's default threshold is conservative: it flags ${pct(M(FX.rf.fpr_default), 2)}% of benign flows, and flagging ${fprTxt} needs a threshold of ${fx(thr("rf"), 2)}. At the matched rate it detects ${pct(mf("rf", "0.1"))}% of attacked flows at ε_{adv} = 0.1, against ${pct(w("rf", "0.1"))}% at its default threshold. The stronger attacks lower every pool-based model's matched recall at 0.5 by ${pct(Math.min(...["knn", "gnn", "fagi_no_dp", "fagi"].map((k) => st(k, "0.5") - mf(k, "0.5"))), 1)}–${pct(Math.max(...["knn", "gnn", "fagi_no_dp", "fagi"].map((k) => st(k, "0.5") - mf(k, "0.5"))), 1)} points relative to the stored attacks alone, and the forest's at 0.1 by ${pp(st("rf", "0.1"), mf("rf", "0.1"))}, but they do not change the ordering of the graph models and the k-NN vote.`));
k.push(P(`The operating point matters most for the DP models. FAGI without DP keeps ${pct(mfx("fagi_no_dp", "0.005", "0.5"))}%, ${pct(mf("fagi_no_dp", "0.5"))}%, and ${pct(mfx("fagi_no_dp", "0.02", "0.5"))}% at false-positive rates of 0.5%, 1%, and 2%; FAGI keeps ${pct(mfx("fagi", "0.005", "0.5"))}%, ${pct(mf("fagi", "0.5"))}%, and ${pct(mfx("fagi", "0.02", "0.5"))}%: ${mfx("fagi", "0.005", "0.5") < mfx("gnn", "0.005", "0.5") ? "slightly below" : "level with"} the GNN at 0.5% (${pct(mfx("gnn", "0.005", "0.5"))}%), level at 1%, and clearly ahead only at 2% (${folds(FT["fagi_vs_gnn_fixed_0.02_0.5"])}). How much of adversarial training's gain DP gives back therefore depends on the operating point: at 0.5% it gives back ${pct((mfx("fagi_no_dp", "0.005", "0.5") - mfx("fagi", "0.005", "0.5")) / (mfx("fagi_no_dp", "0.005", "0.5") - mfx("gnn", "0.005", "0.5")), 0)}% of it, at 1% ${pct((mf("fagi_no_dp", "0.5") - mf("fagi", "0.5")) / (mf("fagi_no_dp", "0.5") - mf("gnn", "0.5")), 0)}%, and at 2% ${pct((mfx("fagi_no_dp", "0.02", "0.5") - mfx("fagi", "0.02", "0.5")) / (mfx("fagi_no_dp", "0.02", "0.5") - mfx("gnn", "0.02", "0.5")), 0)}%. The reason is visible in the thresholds: to flag ${fprTxt} of benign flows, the non-private neural models need a malicious score above ${fx(Math.min(...npNeural.map(thr)), 2)}–${fx(Math.max(...npNeural.map(thr)), 2)} (the k-NN vote ${fx(thr("knn"), 3)}, the random forest ${fx(thr("rf"), 2)}), while FAGI needs ${fx(thr("fagi"), 4)} and the private MLP ${fx(thr("mlp_dp"), 4)}. DP-SGD leaves benign and attacked scores crowded near 1, so small changes in the threshold move many flows across it. Fig. 2 shows that, once false-positive rates are matched, FAGI detects fewer attacked flows than the GNN, the k-NN vote, and FAGI without DP at every budget up to ε_{adv} = 0.2, starting from ${pct(mfx("fagi", "0.01", "0.02"))}% at ε_{adv} = 0.02, and is a little above the GNN at 0.5.`));
k.push(L.figure(path.join(REPO, "figures/fig2_eps_sweep.png"), 3.1, 2.22));
k.push(L.caption(`Fig. 2. Worst-case attack recall at a matched ${fprTxt} false-positive rate on CICIDS2017 as the budget grows (mean ± std over 5 folds × ${nSeeds} seeds; k-NN over fold means).`));

k.push(L.subsection("D. What Privacy Costs and Buys"));
k.push(P(`Fig. 3 varies the noise multiplier. At σ = 1.1, FAGI's guarantee is ε_{DP} = ${fx(eC)}; σ = ${sigOrdered[sigOrdered.length - 1].sigma} gives ε_{DP} = ${fx(M(sigOrdered[sigOrdered.length - 1].epsilon))} at ${pct(sigC[sigC.length - 1], 2)}% clean accuracy, and σ = ${sigOrdered[0].sigma} gives ε_{DP} = ${fx(M(sigOrdered[0].epsilon))} at ${pct(sigC[0], 2)}%, against ${pct(M(noDP.clean), 2)}% without DP. At each model's default threshold, worst-case recall at ε_{adv} = 0.5 stays between ${pct(Math.min(...sigR))}% and ${pct(Math.max(...sigR))}% and is highest at the largest σ, but noisier models also flag more benign flows (${pct(M(FX0["fagi_sigma0.6"].fpr_default), 1)}% at σ = 0.6, ${pct(M(FX0["fagi_sigma4.0"].fpr_default), 1)}% at σ = 4). At a matched ${fprTxt} false-positive rate the trend reverses: recall is ${pct(mf0("fagi_sigma0.6", "0.5"))}% at σ = 0.6, ${pct(mf0("fagi", "0.5"))}% at σ = 1.1, ${pct(mf0("fagi_sigma2.0", "0.5"))}% at σ = 2, and ${pct(mf0("fagi_sigma4.0", "0.5"))}% at σ = 4, against ${pct(mf0("fagi_no_dp", "0.5"))}% without DP. Even at σ = 0.6, where ε_{DP} = ${fx(M(sigOrdered[0].epsilon))} is a weak guarantee, much of the loss is already present, which points to clipping or the training configuration as much as to noise; Section VI-C separates them.  The sweep uses seed 0 only; the comparison at σ = 1.1 is repeated over three seeds in Table III.`));
k.push(L.figure(path.join(REPO, "figures/fig3_privacy_utility.png"), 3.1, 2.39));
k.push(L.caption(`Fig. 3. Privacy-utility trade-off for FAGI on CICIDS2017 (seed 0, 5 folds). Attack recall is at the default threshold except for the lowest curve, which holds each σ to a ${fprTxt} false-positive rate. Gray horizontal lines: FAGI without DP.`));
const med = (v) => { const x = [...v].sort((p, q) => p - q); return x.length % 2 ? x[(x.length - 1) / 2] : (x[x.length / 2 - 1] + x[x.length / 2]) / 2; };
const lossTxt = (v) => `${fx(M(v.mean_loss_members), 3)} and ${fx(M(v.mean_loss_nonmembers), 3)}`;
k.push(P(`Measuring what DP buys is harder. A loss-threshold membership attack {yeom}, run through the deployed retrieval path, scores an AUC between ${fx(Math.min(...Object.values(MIA).map((v) => M(v.auc))), 3)} and ${fx(Math.max(...Object.values(MIA).map((v) => M(v.auc))), 3)} for every model, with and without DP, and its true-positive rate at a 1% false-positive rate (${pct(Math.min(...Object.values(MIA).map((v) => M(v.tpr_at_1pct_fpr))), 1)}–${pct(Math.max(...Object.values(MIA).map((v) => M(v.tpr_at_1pct_fpr))), 1)}%) is what guessing gives. The attack's losses are computed in double precision from the logit margin; in single precision many confidently classified flows tie at a loss of exactly zero, which would force this rate to zero. It also fails on a positive control, a GNN trained for ${cfg.overfit_epochs} epochs on ${int(cfg.overfit_n)} flows (AUC ${fx(M(MIA.gnn_overfit.auc), 3)}, best per-class AUC ${fx(M(MIA.gnn_overfit.max_per_class_auc), 3)}). The reason is visible in the losses: the median loss is ${fx(M(MIA.gnn.median_loss_members), 3)} for members and non-members of every model, and mean losses for members and non-members are ${lossTxt(MIA.gnn)} for the GNN, ${lossTxt(MIA.fagi)} for FAGI, and ${lossTxt(MIA.gnn_overfit)} for the control. Most flows of both kinds are fit almost perfectly, so this attack cannot separate them and its silence says nothing about DP.`));
const canTxt = (x) => `${fx(CAN[x].auc_pooled, 3)} [${fx(CAN[x].auc_pooled_ci95[0], 3)}, ${fx(CAN[x].auc_pooled_ci95[1], 3)}]`;
k.push(P(`A canary audit {jagielski} has more power, and on this data it is our only empirical privacy evidence. We flip the labels of 200 private flows per fold, train with half of them, and ask whether the model's loss separates included from excluded canaries. Pooled over folds (${CAN.fagi.n_in + CAN.fagi.n_out} canaries; bootstrap 95% intervals), the undefended GNN separates them with AUC ${canTxt("gnn")} and FAGI without DP with ${canTxt("fagi_no_dp")}; FAGI with DP-SGD scores ${canTxt("fagi")}, an interval that contains 0.5. Canaries are barely memorized even without DP: ${pct(M(CAN.gnn.fit_rate_in), 1)}% of included canaries reach a loss below log 2 for the GNN, against ${pct(M(CAN.gnn.fit_rate_out), 1)}% of excluded ones, so the signal DP removes is weak. Per fold, FAGI's AUC is ${fx(M(CAN.fagi.auc_per_fold), 3)} ± ${fx(CAN.fagi.auc_per_fold.std, 3)}. Choosing a loss threshold on half of the canaries and applying Clopper-Pearson bounds to its true- and false-positive rates on the other half gives empirical lower bounds on ε of ${fx(CAN.gnn.empirical_eps_lower_bound)} for the GNN, ${fx(CAN.fagi_no_dp.empirical_eps_lower_bound)} for FAGI without DP, and ${fx(CAN.fagi.empirical_eps_lower_bound)} for FAGI, all below the accountant's ε_{DP} = ${fx(CAN.fagi.epsilon)} for the audit models (they train on 100 fewer records), which any bound of this size would be. These use one split fixed in advance; over ten random splits the medians are ${fx(med(CAN.gnn.eps_lower_bound_over_splits.values))} (range ${fx(Math.min(...CAN.gnn.eps_lower_bound_over_splits.values))}–${fx(Math.max(...CAN.gnn.eps_lower_bound_over_splits.values))}) for the GNN and ${fx(med(CAN.fagi_no_dp.eps_lower_bound_over_splits.values))} (${fx(Math.min(...CAN.fagi_no_dp.eps_lower_bound_over_splits.values))}–${fx(Math.max(...CAN.fagi_no_dp.eps_lower_bound_over_splits.values))}) for FAGI without DP, and FAGI's bound is 0 in every split. Each value is a valid bound for its own split; they cannot be combined into a tighter one. The test is one-sided: it looks for included canaries with lower loss, the direction memorization produces. These bounds also treat the canaries of one training run as independent trials; the one-run analysis of Steinke et al. {steinke} accounts for that dependence. The bound is limited by the canaries, not their number: with half of the canaries held out for evaluation, perfect separation would certify about ε ≈ 4.2. Label-flipped flows from the training distribution are surrounded by correctly labeled near-copies and are hard to memorize, so even the non-private models certify at most ${fx(Math.max(...CAN.fagi_no_dp.eps_lower_bound_over_splits.values, ...CAN.gnn.eps_lower_bound_over_splits.values))}. The audit therefore shows that DP-SGD changes how the model treats these canaries, which reduced fitting alone could also produce; it could not detect an implementation error and does not test ε_{DP} = ${fx(eC)}. Out-of-distribution or gradient canaries {steinke} would be needed for that.`));

k.push(L.subsection("E. API-Native Evaluation"));
k.push(...L.tableTitle("IV", "Synthetic API sessions, all attack types (5-fold CV, %)"));
const apiRows = [["rf", "Random forest"], ["gnn_nopayload", "GNN (5 features)"], ["gnn", "GNN (6 features)"], ["fagi_no_payload", "FAGI − payload"], ["fagi", "FAGI"]];
k.push(L.table(["Method", "Original", "Hard", "Hard, PGD"],
  apiRows.map(([key, name]) => ({ bold: key === "fagi", cells: [name, pct(M(A0[key].clean)), `${pct(M(AH[key].clean))} ± ${pct(AH[key].clean.std)}`,
    AH[key].adv ? pct(M(AH[key].adv)) : "n/a"] })), [1700, 900, 1100, 900]));
k.push(L.caption(`FAGI variants train with DP-SGD on ${int(nSynTrain)} sessions per fold (ε_{DP} = ${fx(eS)}). PGD: ε_{adv} = 0.1 in calibration-standard-deviation units on called endpoints. The random forest uses node features and the flattened adjacency, including the payload score. The single-attack runs in the text train on fewer sessions (ε_{DP} = ${fx(M(LMH.fagi.epsilon))}).`, false));
const minOrig = Math.min(M(A0.rf.clean), M(A0.gnn.clean), M(A0.fagi.clean));
k.push(P(`On the original generator every model with the payload score reaches ${minOrig >= 0.9995 ? "100%" : `at least ${(Math.floor(1000 * minOrig) / 10).toFixed(1)}%`} accuracy, so the dataset cannot support claims about payload modeling. Without the score, DP-trained FAGI detects ${pct(SQ0.models.fagi_no_payload.per_type_detection.sql_injection)}% of SQL injection sessions, since nothing else in them differs from benign ones. Table IV gives the accuracies. On the hard generator the payload score is worth ${pp(M(AH.fagi.clean), M(AH.fagi_no_payload.clean))} points to FAGI, and among FAGI's defenses DP is again the largest cost: FAGI reaches ${pct(M(AH.fagi.clean))}% against ${pct(M(AH.gnn.clean))}% for the undefended GNN with the same features. Accuracy hides where that cost lands. In the single-attack lateral-movement task on the hard generator (lateral movement is the only attack type in training; ε_{DP} = ${fx(M(LMH.fagi.epsilon))}), FAGI's accuracy is ${pct(M(LMH.fagi.clean))}% but it detects only ${pct(LMH.fagi.per_type_detection.lateral_movement)}% of lateral-movement sessions, against ${pct(LMH.rf.per_type_detection.lateral_movement)}% for the random forest and ${pct(LMH.gnn_nopayload.per_type_detection.lateral_movement)}% for the five-feature GNN: under DP the model has learned to call almost everything benign.`));
k.push(P(`Random edge injection into hard-generator test graphs changes accuracy by at most ${pct(Math.max(...Object.values(GS.drop_at_80pct).map((v) => M(v))), 2)} points at +80% edges for any model (GNN ${pct(M(GS.drop_at_80pct.gnn), 2)}, FAGI without GNNGuard ${pct(M(GS.drop_at_80pct.fagi_no_guard), 2)}, FAGI ${pct(M(GS.drop_at_80pct.fagi), 2)} points). Random injection is a weak structure attack, and these differences do not establish a benefit from GNNGuard.`));

k.push(L.subsection("F. Federated Generalization"));
k.push(P(`Organizations 0–3 train and organization 4, the only one with all four attack types, is held out. Each training organization keeps 20% of its sessions as non-members. We run ${R.exp4.rounds} rounds of one local epoch, keep each client's Adam state across rounds, and repeat ${R.exp4.repeats} times. Client DP-SGD gives ε_{DP} = ${fx(eF)} per organization, since each holds about ${int(Math.round(nFedMembers / 4))} member sessions; the "ε matched" row trains centrally with σ = ${fx(M(F0.central_dp_eps_matched.sigma))}, chosen so that its ε_{DP} equals the per-client value. The central DP row (ε_{DP} = ${fx(M(F0.central_dp.epsilon))}) differs from Table IV's ${fx(eS)} only because it trains on ${int(nFedMembers)} member sessions instead of ${int(nSynTrain)}.`));
k.push(...L.tableTitle("V", "Accuracy on the held-out organization (%, mean ± std over repeats)"));
k.push(L.table(["Setting", "Original", "Hard", "ε_{DP}"],
  FED_TEXT.rows.map(([key, name]) => ({ bold: key === "fedavg_dp", cells: [name, pm(F0[key].accuracy), pm(FH[key].accuracy),
    F0[key].epsilon ? fx(M(F0[key].epsilon)) : "—"] })), [2050, 1000, 1000, 550]));
k.push(L.caption("Local rows average the four organizations, each trained alone; their ε_{DP} is from their own accountant.", false));
k.push(P(FED_TEXT.body));

k.push(L.subsection("G. File-Order Split"));
k.push(P(TEMP_TEXT.intro));
k.push(...L.tableTitle("VI", "CICIDS2017 file-order split: train on the first 70% of each day file, test on the last 30% (%, mean ± std over 3 seeds)"));
k.push(L.table(["Method", "Clean acc.", "Detection rate", "FPR", "ε_{adv}=0.1", "ε_{adv}=0.5", `ε_{adv}=0.1, ${fprTxt} FPR`],
  TP.models.map((b) => ({ bold: b.key === "fagi", cells: [shortName[b.key] || b.name, pm(b.clean, 2), pct(M(b.detection_rate), 1), pct(M(b.fpr), 2),
    pm(b["worst_recall_eps0.1"]), pm(b["worst_recall_eps0.5"]), FXTm[b.key] ? pct(M(FXTm[b.key]["fixed_0.01_0.1"])) : "—"] })), [1200, 950, 600, 550, 750, 650, 750]));
k.push(L.caption(`Detection rate: share of malicious test flows detected without attack. The k-NN vote has no random state, and its worst case is the same for every seed, so its std is 0. The last column is a mean over seeds. The 1D-CNN was not run on this split. ε_{DP} = ${fx(M(TPB.fagi.epsilon))} for FAGI and ${fx(M(TPB.mlp_dp.epsilon))} for the private MLP, higher than in Table I because the training sets are smaller.`, false));
