k.push(L.section("VII. Discussion"));
k.push(L.subsection("A. What the Evidence Supports"));
k.push(P(`Three findings hold in our cross-validation experiments on CICIDS2017, and one recommendation follows from them. Adversarial training is worth its cost when the attack budget is non-trivial: without DP, it more than doubles the undefended GNN's worst-case recall at ε_{adv} = 0.5 at a matched false-positive rate, under attacks that minimize its score. DP-SGD at σ = 1.1, with the non-private training configuration, costs one to two points of accuracy on CICIDS2017, more on rare classes, much more when organizations hold little data, and most of the robustness adversarial training adds, which a comparison at default thresholds hides. We therefore recommend that robustness claims be made at a matched false-positive rate, and for private models at more than one, because their scores crowd near 1 and their robustness moves with the operating point. And the cheapest model in our study, a k-NN vote over a shared reference pool, which uses no private record, is as robust as FAGI without DP and more robust than FAGI once false-positive rates are matched. ${XC.discussion} Any claim that a graph detector is robust on this benchmark should be checked against that baseline. ${TEMP_TEXT.discussion}`));
k.push(L.subsection("B. Limitations"));
k.push(P(`Our file-order split is within each day file and only roughly chronological. It tests two attack types absent from training (Heartbleed and SQL injection), but it holds out neither whole days nor attack types systematically, and the cross-validation results share near-duplicate flows across the split {arp}. We use the original labels, which have known errors {engelen}, and a sample of ${int(ds.sample_rows)} flows rather than all ${int(ds.clean_rows)}. The attacks work in feature space, and a perturbed flow need not be traffic an attacker can send {apruzzese23,pierazzi,zhan25}; budgets are also applied to features the attacker does not control, such as backward-direction statistics, which makes the attacker stronger than a real one. Our attacks are our own design. Score-based random search adds almost nothing to the gradient attacks, which argues against gradient masking, but the stored adaptive PGD keeps improving with restarts, and a parameter-free suite such as AutoAttack {autoattack} adapted to retrieval would be a stronger check. The random-forest attack is query-based and could be tightened by an exact attack on the tree ensemble. DP noise and Poisson sampling are drawn from a seeded generator for reproducibility, which shares its seed with weight initialization and dropout. Because the seeds are public, the released checkpoints carry no DP guarantee; ε_{DP} describes the mechanism run with fresh, cryptographically secure randomness. Our DP-SGD hyperparameters are mostly the non-private ones, apart from the one alternative configuration in Section VI-C. Exact duplicates and constant columns are removed from the whole dataset before the private/pool split, which the guarantee does not cover. The reference pool is assumed shareable; if it must be private, the node-level methods of {daigavane,gap} apply. The guarantee is per record, while a host contributes many flows. The API data are synthetic and the hard generator is our own design. The payload score is a keyword count; the repository's DistilBERT embedder is not used in any experiment. Federation is simulated in one process, and we evaluate no poisoning defense.`));
k.push(L.subsection("C. Future Work"));
k.push(P(`The next tests this design should face are problem-space attacks that respect protocol constraints {pierazzi,sheatsley}, optimized structure attacks against GNNGuard, splits that hold out whole days or attack types, and host-level privacy accounting.`));

k.push(L.section("VIII. Conclusion"));
k.push(P(`FAGI combines graph detection, adversarial training, edge filtering, and differentially private federated learning, and this paper measures each component instead of assuming it helps. On CICIDS2017, clean detection is easy for every model. Under attacks that minimize each model's score, held to a matched ${fprTxt} false-positive rate, adversarial training keeps the graph detector at ${pct(mf("fagi_no_dp", "0.5"))}% worst-case recall at ε_{adv} = 0.5 where an undefended GNN keeps ${pct(mf("gnn", "0.5"))}%, but with DP-SGD FAGI keeps ${pct(mf("fagi", "0.5"))}%. GNNGuard adds nothing, and an untrained k-NN vote (${pct(mf("knn", "0.5"))}%) matches adversarial training without DP and does better than FAGI. Drawing neighbors from a separate reference pool gives DP-SGD a per-record guarantee for the private half (ε_{DP} = ${fx(eC)}), ${XC.conclusion} DP's cost also falls on the smaller attack classes. Code, data loaders, and the scripts that produce every number in this paper are public.`));

